---
import DataFlowDiagram from '../components/DataFlowDiagram.astro';
import PageBanner from '../components/PageBanner.astro';
import Base, { REPO_URL } from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About — spoodHub"
  description="How spoodHub stores your data, and how to contribute care information."
>
  <PageBanner
    title="About spoodHub"
    subtitle="An open care reference anyone can improve, and a private tracker for your own spiders."
  />

  <section id="how-the-tracker-works" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">How the tracker works</h2>

    <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-info/30 bg-info/10 p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">Your data stays on your device</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm">
        When you visit this site your browser downloads the app itself — some HTML and JavaScript.
        Everything you then log is written to a database inside your own browser (IndexedDB) and
        stays there. There is no account, no server storing your records, and nothing is uploaded.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm">
        This also means other people using this site cannot see your spiders and you cannot see
        theirs. They are separate databases on separate devices that have never been in contact.
      </p>
    </div>

    <DataFlowDiagram />

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">What that costs you</h3>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-muted">
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-alert">▪</span>
        <span>
          <strong class="text-fg">Clearing your browser data deletes your log.</strong> So does
          uninstalling the app, or "clear site data" in browser settings. Download a backup file
          regularly — the tracker will remind you.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-alert">▪</span>
        <span>
          <strong class="text-fg">It does not sync between devices.</strong> Your phone and your
          laptop keep separate logs. To move data between them, download a backup on one and import
          it on the other.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-alert">▪</span>
        <span>
          <strong class="text-fg">Different browsers do not share.</strong> Chrome and Firefox on
          the same phone each get their own database.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">Install it</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      On Android, Chrome's menu offers <em>Add to home screen</em>. On iPhone, use Safari's share
      button then <em>Add to Home Screen</em>. Installing gives you an app icon, an offline-capable
      app, and storage the browser is less likely to clear on its own.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">Enclosure tags</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      Each spider has its own quick-log link, shown on its page in the tracker. Write it to an NFC
      sticker or print it as a QR code and put it on the enclosure — tapping or scanning opens a
      one-tap logging screen for that spider. Because the link points at your own local database,
      it only works on a device that has that spider's record.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">The pre-molt estimate</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      The tracker watches for the signals keepers already use: consecutive refused feedings, a
      sealed silk retreat, heavy webbing, and how far the spider is through its expected molt
      interval. Until a spider has molted twice under your care it uses a rough interval for its
      instar; after that it uses that spider's own history, which is far more accurate.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      It also accounts for the most common false alarm. Being rehoused unsettles a spider and
      commonly costs it a few feedings, which looks identical to pre-molt on paper. So if the
      refusals only started after a rehouse you logged, it says so rather than telling you to stop
      feeding — unless the retreat has sealed up, which is physical evidence that outranks any
      amount of stress.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      It always shows you the reasons behind its verdict, because it is a prompt to go look at the
      enclosure — not a replacement for looking. It can be wrong, and a spider that has genuinely
      stopped eating for another reason deserves attention rather than a stopped feeding schedule.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contributing" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Contributing care information</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      Every species profile and care guide on this site is a Markdown file in the repository. If you
      keep jumping spiders and something here is wrong, incomplete, or missing a species, the fix is
      a pull request — you do not need to know how the app works.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a
        href={REPO_URL}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg bg-accent px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-accent-fg transition hover:opacity-90"
      >
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" class="size-4 fill-current">
          <path
            d="M8 0C3.58 0 0 3.58 0 8c0 3.54 2.29 6.53 5.47 7.59.4.07.55-.17.55-.38 0-.19-.01-.82-.01-1.49-2.01.37-2.53-.49-2.69-.94-.09-.23-.48-.94-.82-1.13-.28-.15-.68-.52-.01-.53.63-.01 1.08.58 1.23.82.72 1.21 1.87.87 2.33.66.07-.52.28-.87.51-1.07-1.78-.2-3.64-.89-3.64-3.95 0-.87.31-1.59.82-2.15-.08-.2-.36-1.02.08-2.12 0 0 .67-.21 2.2.82.64-.18 1.32-.27 2-.27s1.36.09 2 .27c1.53-1.04 2.2-.82 2.2-.82.44 1.1.16 1.92.08 2.12.51.56.82 1.27.82 2.15 0 3.07-1.87 3.75-3.65 3.95.29.25.54.73.54 1.48 0 1.07-.01 1.93-.01 2.2 0 .21.15.46.55.38A8.01 8.01 0 0 0 16 8c0-4.42-3.58-8-8-8Z"
          />
        </svg>
        View the repository
      </a>
      <a
        href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-raised px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium transition hover:border-accent/50"
      >
        Full contributing guide
      </a>
      <a
        href={`${REPO_URL}/issues/new`}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-raised px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium transition hover:border-accent/50"
      >
        Report a problem
      </a>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
      No GitHub account? Opening an issue only takes a free one, and reporting something wrong is
      just as useful as fixing it yourself.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-4">
        <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-accent">1</div>
        <h3 class="mt-1 font-medium">Find the file</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">
          Species live in <code class="text-xs">src/content/species/</code>, guides in{' '}
          <code class="text-xs">src/content/care/</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-4">
        <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-accent">2</div>
        <h3 class="mt-1 font-medium">Edit and open a PR</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">
          GitHub's pencil button works fine. At least one source is required — husbandry folklore
          spreads fast, and the build rejects a page that cites nothing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-4">
        <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-accent">3</div>
        <h3 class="mt-1 font-medium">Checks run automatically</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">
          A missing or malformed field fails the build and shows up on your PR before a human looks
          at it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">What a species file looks like</h3>
    <pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-4 text-xs"><code>---
commonName: Regal Jumping Spider
scientificName: Phidippus regius
nativeRange: Southeastern United States, the Bahamas and the Greater Antilles
adultSize:
  female: 15–22 mm
  male: 12–18 mm
lifespan:
  female: 18–24 months
  male: 12–18 months
temperament: bold          # bold | shy | skittish | variable
difficulty: beginner       # beginner | intermediate | advanced
temperatureC:
  min: 21
  max: 27
humidityPct:
  min: 50
  max: 70

# At least one source is required, and each is tagged with what kind of
# evidence it is. The tag is shown to readers next to the link.
sources:
  - label: World Spider Catalog — Phidippus regius (C. L. Koch, 1846)
    url: https://wsc.nmbe.ch/species/31858/Phidippus_regius
    kind: database         # paper | book | database | institution | care-sheet | community

# Optional. Your own observations go here rather than in the text above,
# and render in a separate "From keepers" block credited to you.
tips:
  - text: Mine refuses blue bottles below about 21 °C but takes them at 24 °C.
    by: your-name
    context: Two females, over about a year

updated: 2026-08-21
---

Everything below the dashes is normal Markdown, and becomes the body of the page.</code></pre>

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">Species with no published care data</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      Most of the roughly 7,000 jumping spider species have never been kept or written up. A
      profile can declare that with <code class="text-xs">careData: undocumented</code>, which
      drops the husbandry fields and shows a visible "Husbandry not documented" notice instead. A
      reader can then tell <em>nobody knows</em> from <em>nobody bothered</em>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      Such a profile still has to earn its place. The build accepts it only if you can show one of
      two things: <strong class="text-fg">firsthand experience</strong> — at least one tip and a
      photo, which you cannot produce for a spider you do not have — or{' '}
      <strong class="text-fg">published research</strong>, meaning a peer-reviewed source, which is
      why the two species studied in the sleep and behavior guides are here.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      A taxonomic database entry on its own is deliberately not enough. Otherwise anyone could
      generate hundreds of stubs from a species list, and this would stop being a keeper's care
      reference and turn into a catalogue.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">Photos</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      A species profile can carry one photo of each sex, and those are among the most useful
      contributions there are — jumping spiders are often dramatically different between males and
      females, and a keeper trying to sex a spider learns more from two pictures than from any
      description.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      Every photo needs a <strong class="text-fg">credit, a licence, and a link to where it came
      from</strong>. These are not optional: most usable spider photography is Creative Commons and
      obliges attribution, so the build rejects an image that lacks them rather than leaving it for
      a reviewer to notice. Only add photos you have the right to use — your own, or something
      under a licence that permits it.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold">Species link themselves</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      When a care guide mentions a jumping spider that has a profile on this site, the first
      mention becomes a link to it automatically. You do not write the link yourself —{' '}
      <em>Phidippus regius</em>, <em>P. regius</em> and "Regal Jumping Spider" all work.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      Only species that already have a profile are linked, so a mention of something not yet
      written up stays as plain text rather than turning into a broken link. If a guide you are
      writing references a jumping spider we do not cover yet, adding even a short profile for it
      is what keeps the guides connected to one another.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      Spiders that are not jumping spiders are never linked this way. The guides also mention{' '}
      <em>Parasteatoda tepidariorum</em>, a cobweb spider cited for pedipalp development, and{' '}
      <em>Drosophila</em> fruit flies as prey — neither belongs in the species section.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-8 text-muted">
      The full field list and rules live in{' '}
      <code class="text-xs">CONTRIBUTING.md</code> in the repository.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-watch/30 bg-watch/10 p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">A note on accuracy</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm">
        Invertebrate husbandry advice online is often repeated without a source and sometimes
        actively harmful. Prefer published care sheets, peer-reviewed papers, and firsthand keeper
        experience described as such. If something is contested, say so on the page rather than
        picking a side.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
